    "evidence_text": "We therefore examined risk in relation to plasma concentrations of these vitamin D metabolites.   \n\n\ngamma-tocopherol, the major form of vitamin E in the US diet, deserves more attention.\n\nSome human and animal studies indicate that plasma concentrations of gamma-tocopherol are inversely associated with the incidence of cardiovascular disease and prostate cancer.\n\nTaken together, these findings strengthen the case for consideration of long-term aspirin use in CRC prevention.\n\nCancer chemoprevention: a rapidly evolving field\n\nNo previous trial in men at usual risk has examined vitamin C alone in the prevention of cancer.   \n\n\nOBJECTIVE To evaluate whether long-term vitamin E or C supplementation decreases risk of prostate and total cancer events among men.   \n\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Prostate and total cancer.   \n\n\nThese data provide no support for the use of these supplements for the prevention of cancer in middle-aged and older men.   \n\n\nVitamin D and prevention of colorectal cancer.\n\nPlasma lipids and prolactin in patients with breast cancer.\n\nHDL-Cholesterol levels were significantly lower in these patients.\n\nPreventing coronary heart disease: B vitamins and homocysteine.\n\n)  \n\nGreat successes have been achieved, especially for breast and colorectal cancer.",
